8
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:09,520
On the podcast, we talk with Vince about the right way to raise prices, the painful lessons from picking the wrong tools, and why you should respond to every single app review.

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,280
Vince, thank you so much for joining us on the podcast today.

10
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:14,320
I can't wait to chat.

11
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,160
Oh, I'm so honored to be here.

12
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:19,200
I'm a long time listener of the podcast, so I'm a huge fan.

13
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:22,400
So it's amazing that I get to sit here and be on the cast with you.

14
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:23,040
Awesome.

15
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,440
And Jacob, freshly shorn.

16
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,720
You look like a baby without your three foot beard.

17
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,360
Nobody respects me now because I'm cleanly shaven.

18
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:35,280
I look like I'm thirteen, but I'll take it.

19
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:36,080
I'll take it.

20
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:37,760
It's nice to be back, everybody.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:38,800
I'm the same person.

22
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,040
Today someone on the street stopped me.

23
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:41,680
I don't even know.

24
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:42,960
And she was like, Oh, you've shaved.

25
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:43,920
I was like, Who are you?

26
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:45,440
That's pretty good.

27
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:51,200
For those of you listening to the audio podcast, you'll have to check out YouTube and see the new and improved Jacob.

28
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:51,440
Yeah.

29
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:51,760
Okay.

30
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:07,760
So Vince, I wanted to kick things off talking about the story of talking parents and how you went from a consultancy where you were grinding for that next client and just always doing work for other people, always billing hourly or project.

31
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:13,280
And the way you told it to me when we were chatting before the podcast is that you wanted to make money while you slept.

32
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:26,080
And it's pretty great that you went from a consultancy where you were just grinding for those next clients to a ten million dollar year ARR business, which is just incredible.

33
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:34,320
So start me off with what was that motivation and how did you start that process of going from an agency to being a product company?

34
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:40,000
So my business partner, Lewis Erickson, we've been best friends since high school, so we've known each other a long time.

35
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:43,920
We kind of decided we wanted to go off and start doing custom software development.

36
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:48,800
And so we developed this professional services organization about year nine.

37
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:50,240
We made Inc.

38
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:54,640
5000 list and we kind of realized that we had a business now.

39
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,800
We didn't have a hobby anymore and that we really needed to think differently.

40
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:07,120
We met this guy named Steven Nixon and Steven had graduated from law school and was a private practice as an attorney.

41
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:08,720
He had been a prosecutor.

42
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:14,480
He had been a defense attorney and he was in this practice with his brother and his brother was a family law attorney.

43
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:34,400
So Steven shows up in court after his brother gets tapped to be a magistrate and there's two co parents and they're going back and forth over this record of communications which once got text messages in one hand, a Facebook post and emails and they're going back and for forth and the judge is annoyed and both advocates are racking up hours.

44
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:40,800
And so the judge finally throws it back and says, Okay, you guys go off on yourselves and you guys work this out and then come back to me.

45
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:44,000
So Steven sort of came up with this idea to build this app.

46
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:50,080
So he hired some guy working out of his house and I had him build it and then got about a thousand people using it.

47
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,880
And so he realized, Hey, this is on servers inside this guy's house.

48
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,280
And when I call him, he's not always responsive.

49
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:04,720
So he begins to get worried because judges are ordering people onto this software and he's afraid that it's going to come tumbling down or something.

50
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:10,400
So he stops into the Bank of America building where we're at and says, Hey, I want you to take a look at this app.

51
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:15,360
We go in and we look at this app and we come back and we say, Hey man, this wasn't built to scale.

52
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,600
It's kind of held together with baling wire and duct tape, but we think the idea is really cool.

53
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:26,320
What would you think about us forming a whole new company and let's rebuild this thing so that it will scale?

54
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:36,160
And so we came together and started to figure out how we use the professional services to mutually support the app and then build the app up and take it from there.

55
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:44,320
How did that go, transitioning from thinking like a comp hired gun to now we've got this product.

56
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:51,120
I think there's a lot of agencies out there who try and make the jump, but it really is a different skill set.

57
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:55,680
How did that part of the transition go for the Bitwizards now Talking Parents crew?

58
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:11,120
It's a difficult transition and I will tell you for the first couple of years, we didn't have the app monetized as far as a product we were thinking about, you know, when you build custom software, you're just building things and getting them done and you're pulling in pieces and putting it together.

59
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:15,040
It may or may not need to scale to the level that this app did.

60
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:22,560
And so when we started bringing in third party components and stuff like that, we really hadn't thought through all the things in the very beginning.

61
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:32,880
And that created some technical debt and really got us to start reworking things that we had already done before as we were trying to get that right product market fit.

62
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:40,160
And I think the other thing that was different for us was we didn't really understand who our customer was in the beginning.

63
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:41,440
You're an agency.

64
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:45,360
You're used to having a customer who tells you what to do.

65
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:47,360
You're not as deep in the product.

66
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,040
You're not having to think who's our customer.

67
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:53,360
I mean, you know who your customer is the person who's short on software development, right?

68
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:55,600
That's ultimately, I mean, your customer, right?

69
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:04,480
Surely you need to align your goals with theirs, but even the best person who wants to do the best for everyone is going to follow their most direct incentive, which is very different.

70
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:11,680
I was wondering, like, before you had this opportunity land in your lap, was this a goal for you to bring in to like own your own product?

71
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,120
And this was just an opportunity?

72
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:17,440
It just was like right time, right moment, like, let's do this, or had you tried to do this transition?

73
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:20,320
Was this like a strategic thing or just something like opportunistic?

74
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,480
When you're in a custom software business, everyone comes to you and says, hey, I got an idea.

75
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:27,920
And we want you to build it for us, but we don't have any money.

76
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:30,880
So you build it and we'll give you a piece of it.

77
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:37,360
And we quickly learned that our job was to be software engineers and not figure out someone's business model or how they were going to work.

78
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:40,880
But we did have a couple of things that we had attempted.

79
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:45,120
One of them was a product where we became the sole reseller of it online.

80
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:50,640
We built the website, did the stuff, but it was a actual physical product that had to be shipped.

81
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:52,240
It was called Tailgator Carrier.

82
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:54,400
And so we did that for a couple of years.

83
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:56,160
We invested and it didn't work out.

84
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:59,440
We had to basically shut it down working with another group of guys.

85
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:07,440
And we knew we were just looking for that right thing that seemed like it made the right fit and that we could take and make money while we're sleeping.

86
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:07,840
Yeah.

87
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:12,000
I mean, I don't know all the details, but when he came to you and he says like this thing's getting out of control.

88
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:16,480
I can't handle the demand that you know, that's probably already product market fit, right?

89
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:17,840
I get the point, right?

90
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,480
Like people always ask like, how do I know I have product market fit?

91
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:21,520
And there's varying levels.

92
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:23,760
There's different definitions of market, right?

93
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,360
And how well the fit is in there.

94
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:30,880
But, you know, that moment when the product is growing despite all the problems, right?

95
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:33,520
That's usually the definition that there's something there.

96
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,120
So whether you consciously knew it or not.

97
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:41,680
And it sounds like there was probably a natural limit to where this would go without your expertise, right?

98
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:43,520
Without some real engineering.

99
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:44,800
I mean, I think it's really hard.

100
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:59,280
This echoes against advice I've gotten and heard from, you know, investors in my world and stuff about it's just really hard to own a product and build a product without in-house engineering, without being someone in the driver's seat who's technical enough to drive the product forward.

101
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:05,680
And this is a good example of where somebody was short on that expertise and probably would have been limited in how far they could take it.

102
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:06,160
Yeah.

103
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:10,400
And I think besides just the product market fit and understanding and how to help the market.

104
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:13,520
And having someone that could do the software engineering and work right there with you.

105
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:17,520
I think another side of it was that Lewis and I had already taken a lot of risk.

106
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:20,320
Our business had been in business for ten years at that point.

107
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:23,040
Now we're at 25 years for Bitwizards.

108
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:29,520
And Steven came from the law arena where they're all in the risk mitigation business, right?

109
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:35,680
And so you had a couple of entrepreneurs and people that understood that aspect of it and what it meant to take risks.

110
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:42,480
And we were able to work with Steven and sort of get him out of his comfort zone so that we could scale this and make it go well.

111
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:44,320
So it was really mutually beneficial.

112
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:46,080
We really all fit in together well.

113
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:52,240
But I will tell you, we thought we understood who our customer was in the beginning, but we really didn't.

114
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:57,040
And so that's kind of a good segue because we thought our customer was the court system.

115
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:59,840
Yeah, good luck selling SAS to the court system.

116
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:02,640
Well, at least you know where they are.

117
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:09,040
But if you go knocking on their door, they don't look kindly upon that, I guess is probably also true.

118
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:12,560
Well, and they also only want to talk to other people in their profession.

119
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:15,360
You know, it's kind of like a lot of regulated industries.

120
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:18,080
They've got older technology and things like that.

121
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:20,000
But we knew that they were a channel.

122
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:27,600
So initially, we made the application free and Steven thought we were going to monetize through Google AdSense.

123
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:29,920
And so we came to the conclusion pretty quickly.

124
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:33,120
I said, hey, look, everybody in that courtroom's making money.

125
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,120
We've got to make money to scale this thing so that we can fuel it and build the features and functions that the customers want.

126
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:43,040
But our customer isn't the court system.

127
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:50,160
So we changed and decided to monetize based upon the apps and get a subscription model going.

128
00:09:50,960 --> 00:09:56,400
And when we did that and put the focus in the customer and the people that were actually giving us money.

129
00:09:56,720 --> 00:10:02,080
In fact, I had a professor at Notre Dame that told me, he said, strategically, you know who it is.

130
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:04,080
They're the person that gives you money.

131
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:06,480
They're the ones that are in the driver's seat.

132
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:13,680
So it's amazing how we have to sometimes we have to go learn these lessons from fancy professors, but it's like, absolutely.

133
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,360
It's like right in front of you, right?

134
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:18,400
So when you say customers, you're talking about the parents, right?

135
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:20,880
Like the folks that are in a coparenting arrangement.

136
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:22,240
That's absolutely correct.

137
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:28,320
In those early days when you introduced a subscription, you still did use a freemium model, right?

138
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:35,520
So tell me about your decision making around, okay, this is never going to scale big enough to make meaningful money on ads.

139
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:38,080
We're going to use subscriptions, but we need some hook.

140
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:40,640
So how did you think about freemium early?

141
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:45,040
And then I'd actually want to go through the different phases of your freemium strategy as well.

142
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:54,800
Well, Steven thought that the basic messaging part of what we were doing was the core item and we were already giving that away for free through the freemium model.

143
00:10:55,120 --> 00:11:01,280
So we said, you know, when we decided to monetize, you got to remember this is zero nine zero ten eleven, right?

144
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:02,640
And apps are coming out.

145
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:09,520
And so we're like, okay, so we're going to build some some apps and apps have notifications, which you didn't really have them very well over the web.

146
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:11,040
So it started as a web app.

147
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,440
It was like a full web experience before.

148
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:13,680
Okay.

149
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:14,640
Exactly.

150
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:23,840
And so we said, okay, so now we're going to give the mobile apps and the incentive is going to be, well, if you want to use mobile apps, then we're going to charge you for that on a subscription basis.

151
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:30,320
And there was some discussion that went back and forth about whether we're going to charge a onetime fee and what the price point was.

152
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:36,240
And I mean, at that point in time, it was like the market wasn't really there for charging consumer subscriptions on mobile.

153
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:42,320
Like, I don't even think that technically had any subscriptions on the app store until eleven or twelve, until it was, everybody was able to use it.

154
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:42,800
Yeah.

155
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:46,800
And so we went through a lot of arguments back and forth about how to do that.

156
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:55,520
And then when we finally did get it monetized and get it on a subscription, it was closer to 2015 when we finally got it working the way it needed to work.

157
00:11:56,160 --> 00:12:01,200
So we probably burned through close to a million dollars, revamping the app, starting the market.

158
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:07,520
We were literally sending Steven out around the country on a plane to talk to judges and clerks of court and stuff.

159
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:09,680
Was you not monetizing at all during that period?

160
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:10,800
No, not really.

161
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:18,560
All we were doing then, we didn't really start monetizing until the end of 2015, beginning of 2016, other than through the Google AdSense revenue.

162
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:20,480
Oh, so you did have a little bit of that.

163
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:25,760
I mean, normally AdSense as a strategy or ads as a strategy doesn't really work unless you're mass, mass market.

164
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,880
But then I was thinking like, this kind of could be mass, mass market, right?

165
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:34,480
Like there must be millions of people in this arrangement, right, that could potentially use a coparenting app.

166
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:36,640
Yeah, and they weren't necessarily divorced.

167
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:39,280
They could have had a kid together and never been married.

168
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:46,800
It's got to be a bigger market than, I mean, everybody could probably take more than two hands to name people they know in this situation, right?

169
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,360
Which is a larger addressable market than a lot of.

170
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,080
apps we talk to, right?

171
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,280
So you had that going for you.

172
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:58,000
I don't think it was totally crazy, but you guys were pouring RD budget into this thing.

173
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:00,960
Like, were you like pouring effort into the app for those five years?

174
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:06,240
Like, as you were just building functionality, was it kind of on the back burner or like, how were you approaching it?

175
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:21,360
We first revamped the web app and then we went in and started doing the mobile apps on the latter part after a lot of discussion and some of the things that we did early on that were really important, we started asking customers, at least surveying them and saying, okay, what do you want?

176
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:22,480
What would you pay for?

177
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:24,160
What features or functions do you like?

178
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:26,640
How can this be better for you?

179
00:13:27,360 --> 00:13:30,320
It was pretty rudimentary, nothing like what's available today.

180
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:37,680
It was just simply a couple of software engineers and a legal guy sitting in a room saying, Okay, well, what kind of questions do we want to ask our customers?

181
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:41,680
So we started to get that survey data back and figure out and solidify what we wanted.

182
00:13:42,000 --> 00:14:03,280
And when we released the first app, I personally felt like we were charging too little for it, which caused some problems later on because as we started to add more value and more functionality and figure out how to build out our tiers and give customers a compelling reason to not only utilize the app but stay on, we had to do deal with some of that legacy stuff that we were doing from before.

183
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:08,640
When your app is free, now you're charging them 499 and then later on you want to charge them 999.

184
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:12,320
And now we have two tiers where it's 999 and 2499.

185
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:15,280
And so how do you manage customer expectations?

186
00:14:15,360 --> 00:14:21,840
How do you deliver the value to them so that they feel like they're getting something unique and that they're willing to continue to pay for and stay with you?

187
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:31,760
When the app had fewer features and didn't have as big an audience and product market, do you not think maybe it was the right option to start at a lower price?

188
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,640
So you're talking about tech debt in that the price was lower.

189
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:37,520
and having to transition to a higher price.

190
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:43,920
It's kind of a combination of tech debt because you have to manage the subscriptions and like business debt of like we used to be a different app.

191
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:45,440
Now we've added all these features.

192
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:56,800
But do you think maybe that was actually a better staging of the app to start low and then move forward versus do you think you could have gotten $25 a month in those early days?

193
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:00,080
Like is the regret that you should have been charging $25 from the beginning?

194
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:01,440
Do you think you really could have done that?

195
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:03,440
I think we could have monetized sooner.

196
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:08,560
I'd like to tell you that what we did was genius and it all worked out the way we wanted it to be.

197
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:13,520
That's what you do when you're taking your victory lap, that it's all clear the whole time through your genius.

198
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:21,680
But the reality is, is that we stumbled through it and we just made mistakes and, you know, there was a lot of argument and discussion and back and forth.

199
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:27,920
And there were some lean times where, you know, I had to tell Stephen at one point, I said, hey, I can't continue to pay you a salary right now.

200
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:30,800
Times are lean in the professional services realm.

201
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:35,840
So, monetization has to be a strategy and has to be something important that we're doing.

202
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:48,480
And I would say that from 2009 to probably 2015, although we rebuilt the web app, we dabbled and it really wasn't until  15 that we got things monetized and really started getting them going.

203
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:52,720
And then we started adding in more features and that type of stuff.

204
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:53,680
It was stressful.

205
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:55,680
They got better as we made money.

206
00:15:55,920 --> 00:16:04,800
You know, they say money can solve problems when you're pulling up by your bootstraps, it does, and the smart use of that money and making the right bets is what's important.

207
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:14,560
On the point of starting low and going high versus starting high and going low, the advice I always give is start high because like the worst case scenario is nobody buys it, right?

208
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:16,880
And then you're giving people a discount, right?

209
00:16:17,280 --> 00:16:19,520
Versus the flip side is you give everyone some price.

210
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:21,360
that's way below the true value.

211
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:29,040
You've now set the expectation that, like, hey, this is only worth 499 a month, which I would imagine is a fairly high retention user base like yours.

212
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:32,480
Like for some apps that are churn and burn, I use the word churn and burn with love, right?

213
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:40,720
Apps that maybe don't like focusing on super long term retention, like you can more freely experiment with prices because your customers are kind of cycling in and out.

214
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:45,120
But for an app where the expectation might be that someone uses you for eighteen years, right?

215
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:49,840
Or some really long period of time, setting that expectation matters, right?

216
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,080
Because you're going to have that customer relationship for a while.

217
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:56,720
So yeah, normally I'm like, oh, just respect your existing users, but feel free to mess around.

218
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:00,080
But in a case where you might have a longer term base, like you might want to be more careful.

219
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:02,400
I just find it's always easier to make things cheaper.

220
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:03,840
Like nobody gets angry on you, right?

221
00:17:04,080 --> 00:17:08,320
You're going to have the same amount of tech debt and business debt by like reducing a price.

222
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:15,200
You're going to lose money potentially from existing users if you bring them down, but like at least the emotional labor is less, right?

223
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:19,040
When you're like lowering the price and you're still trying to find that optimum, right?

224
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:20,240
You're still solving the same problem.

225
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:22,480
You're just coming from a different starting point on the curve.

226
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:28,800
But when you have like really deep product market fit and you've built out all those features.

227
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:32,960
Then you do have very few people who complain when you raise the price.

228
00:17:33,040 --> 00:17:39,200
And that's the next part of the story I wanted to get to is that you did end up raising the price on all of your existing subscribers.

229
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:42,720
Tell me about how you planned that and then how did it go?

230
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:51,120
Again, at the beginning, we didn't have a lot of instrumentation and we weren't really sure to Jacob's point, the people that are with us are predominantly court ordered.

231
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:58,480
So raising prices when it's already low is tricky because they're already mad because they're typically in a co parenting relationship.

232
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:00,240
Not all, but some of them are.

233
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:03,440
So it's a very delicate matter to raise prices and go forward.

234
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:04,480
We were actually surprised.

235
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:12,400
We said, okay, well, if we lose half of our customers as we raise the price from $499 to $999, what is that going to do to our revenue?

236
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:13,120
What's going to happen?

237
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:19,120
And I've been to some pricing seminars and stuff like that and talked to some different people and I said, there's no way that's going to happen.

238
00:18:19,360 --> 00:18:19,520
Right?

239
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:31,360
So we just kind of planned that we would lose at least half and what we're presently surprised is that we got a few complaints, but actually people understood that when we rolled it out, we added additional value.

240
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:43,680
So when we first rolled it out, our $999 plan, we added in what we call accountable calling, which allowed people to be able to make phone calls and have it transcribed and made a part of the record.

241
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:49,120
So it was more than just the messaging that was going back and forth over or keying it in.

242
00:18:49,440 --> 00:19:01,840
And so every time we've raised prices or every time we've busted out to tiers, we've made sure that we've bundled that with a group of value that somebody would get that the customers had asked for from us surveying them.

243
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:07,600
So we could say, hey, look, yes, we're raising prices, but we're bringing you new features and functions that you asked us for.

244
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:12,720
And we're listening to what you're having to say and we're making sure that it's of value to you.

245
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:14,640
So it turns out we made a difference.

246
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:32,960
We maybe lost a quarter or so and maybe some of that was transitional, while they were mad and then they came back because some of them, and I would probably venture to say it's probably about a 60/40 split, about 60% are court ordered, about 40% are not, but we always offered a free version.

247
00:19:33,120 --> 00:19:36,080
If you wanted to use the web version, you could get it for free.

248
00:19:36,240 --> 00:19:44,800
So we said, okay, if you don't want to pay for the extra features here, you can always go back to the free version and you can And that satisfies court orders in most cases?

249
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:45,520
Yes, sir.

250
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:54,640
That's a huge lesson right there though that I think a lot of subscription apps now that you can more easily raise prices are not heating.

251
00:19:55,040 --> 00:20:09,280
If you're Netflix, yeah, you can like increase the price twenty five percent just because you're Netflix and people are going to stay subscribed, but bundling any price raise with like clear additional value, I think was I mean, that's just for startup and probably a big product marketing, right?

252
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:10,320
It's like you probably already should.

253
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:12,480
There's a lot of apps that are I know.

254
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:13,760
Prices and not do it.

255
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:24,320
I mean, it goes back to, um, you know, something we've been thinking about revenue cut a lot recently, just like negotiation tactics and like as we're closing deals, like how do we create non controversial interactions with customers as we're trying to come to a partnership?

256
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:28,960
agreement and there's certain ways you can present, you know, it's pricing, it's all pricing, right?

257
00:20:29,120 --> 00:20:31,760
It's like presentation of pricing, it's so psychological already.

258
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:36,400
You have as an app developer a lot of surface area to influence that psychological experience.

259
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:43,200
If you say like increased price, you know, an increased price with an increase in delivered value is not actually a net loss for people, right?

260
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:47,760
They can opt out on a liquidity basis, like that surplus value for me, I do not need, right?

261
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:49,200
And they can choose to opt out.

262
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:56,640
But, you know, in most cases, you know, if you're surveying users and what you think of is good, then what you're building is hitting their needs.

263
00:20:56,880 --> 00:20:57,920
That's not going to be the case, right?

264
00:20:58,080 --> 00:20:59,280
When were you all doing this?

