391
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:48,720
So all someone has to do is come to our site once or one of our landing pages and then we can track their history and what they're doing over time.

392
00:31:49,280 --> 00:31:53,040
And then we know when they convert and what that journey looks like for them.

393
00:31:53,680 --> 00:32:02,800
Deciding on Bloomreach and doing a bake-off can be a challenging prospect with engineering, battling with marketing, battling with the business team.

394
00:32:03,200 --> 00:32:06,640
And yeah, pricing and who's going to lead and all that kind of stuff.

395
00:32:07,200 --> 00:32:15,600
How has that gone as you've evolved the tooling, like going from one tool to another and figuring out what's actually going to work best for your business?

396
00:32:16,400 --> 00:32:18,640
We've made some mistakes as we've gone through this.

397
00:32:19,040 --> 00:32:31,120
Um, being software engineers and, you know, having an attorney as part of the company being a little more risk averse on things, you know, we always looked at what was the cheapest and what was the easiest to integrate, right?

398
00:32:31,840 --> 00:32:36,960
From a software engineering perspective and cost, there's a couple of instances where that kind of burned us.

399
00:32:37,200 --> 00:32:38,400
And so one of the is.

400
00:32:38,400 --> 00:32:56,160
One of them is that we have an audio translation service and the company that we use now is called Assembly AI, but we had a previous one that was a startup company and we didn't pay particular attention to the unit economics there and we didn't pay particular attention to how it might scale.

401
00:32:57,200 --> 00:33:10,800
And so what ended up happening is that we started having problems with transcription and we started having disconnections from their service and we had to go back and we found that cheaper isn't always better, right?

402
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:28,800
So we actually made this transition to Assembly., we cut our cost in half and we got more features and functionality out of it because instead of they would charge us for each channel that we were transcribing, whereas under the new system it charge us under dual channel and allowed us to go through.

403
00:33:29,040 --> 00:33:52,320
So the learning from that is to make sure that you understand clearly what the unit economics are, what it's going to do to your cost of goods sold and to make sure that you're not, you know, from a software engineering point, it sounds cool that we're going to hook up with another startup company, but if they can't scale when you're scaling, you've just added a problem to your scaling and we've had to go back and re engineer as a result of that.

404
00:33:52,640 --> 00:33:54,240
And it's happened in more than one occasion.

405
00:33:54,640 --> 00:33:58,000
I have to imagine on all the I mean, this assembly AI sounds like it's probably new.

406
00:33:58,160 --> 00:34:06,000
I'd imagine the costs are going to come down on transcription and stuff right now because there's just so much innovation in AI audio video stuff right now.

407
00:34:06,480 --> 00:34:11,280
There is plus it handles multilinguage, which is another feature and function that we want to put in.

408
00:34:11,440 --> 00:34:12,640
But I have to put some props in there.

409
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:18,400
You know, RevenueCat was the first tooling that we integrated to basically help us manage our subscriptions.

410
00:34:18,640 --> 00:34:22,720
We tried to build that ourselves and it was a huge mistake and it was a debacle.

411
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:28,720
We were running around chasing our tail every time Apple would change something or every time Google would change something.

412
00:34:28,960 --> 00:34:29,680
It was just a nightmare.

413
00:34:29,920 --> 00:34:31,280
Now we chase our tails for you.

414
00:34:31,600 --> 00:34:32,240
That's how it works.

415
00:34:32,560 --> 00:34:32,880
Exactly.

416
00:34:33,280 --> 00:34:34,800
And the price doesn't go up when that happens.

417
00:34:34,960 --> 00:34:35,760
So that's the benefit.

418
00:34:35,840 --> 00:34:36,560
It's derisked.

419
00:34:37,120 --> 00:34:37,440
Exactly.

420
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:39,920
Well, I mean, you scale with us as we scale.

421
00:34:40,160 --> 00:34:43,200
And so that's what I'm talking about, paying attention to how that works.

422
00:34:43,440 --> 00:34:44,240
And that was the first.

423
00:34:44,480 --> 00:34:45,760
And then I mentioned Assembly.

424
00:34:46,240 --> 00:34:50,160
The other one was when we had another startup that we worked with to help us with our video.

425
00:34:50,480 --> 00:34:53,360
Right when we're doing our calling, it was our flagship feature.

426
00:34:54,240 --> 00:34:58,320
We underestimated a little bit what the cost was going to be to store the video over the long term.

427
00:34:59,520 --> 00:35:05,520
We have some unique things with the coparents that we want to mask location and that type of thing and mask the phone numbers.

428
00:35:05,840 --> 00:35:08,960
And, you know, we have issues of trying to mix all that together.

429
00:35:09,760 --> 00:35:12,400
And so we went with this other company and they were great.

430
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:23,280
They're really nice, but we started having problems with our flagship feature and we had compared them to Twilio and we decided we're going to go back with this new startup and we're going to help them out.

431
00:35:23,440 --> 00:35:25,040
We're going to work with us and go forward.

432
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:27,200
But the reality is they couldn't scale with us.

433
00:35:28,080 --> 00:35:32,960
And so we ended up making the transition to Twilio and the advantage there is now.

434
00:35:33,680 --> 00:35:37,360
I have a global network at scale that I can actually integrate in with.

435
00:35:37,680 --> 00:35:44,720
So I would tell other founders, if you're a software engineer by trade, you know, your tendency is I want to build it myself.

436
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:47,120
And the answer is don't try to build it yourself.

437
00:35:47,440 --> 00:35:54,080
You find it best in class, don't chance on the money and make sure you pay particular attention to the unit economics.

438
00:35:54,320 --> 00:36:01,600
The other thing that I would say that is really important is I tell my engineers all the time, why are you spinning your heels on that?

439
00:36:02,160 --> 00:36:05,120
Call RevenueCat or call Twilio, right?

440
00:36:05,600 --> 00:36:07,280
This is why we pay for support, right?

441
00:36:07,840 --> 00:36:09,600
These are the guys who are the experts in their product.

442
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:10,960
Let's utilize that.

443
00:36:11,120 --> 00:36:21,440
And I don't mind paying some extra money to be able to have that because if I do that, then my engineers are off developing new features or functions for my, you know, customers that have a higher ROI.

444
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:24,880
I think this is something that I'm glad it sounds like you guys have made that transition.

445
00:36:25,040 --> 00:36:30,800
I see a lot of companies that are, I don't want to describe it, they're somewhat dogmatic about it, right?

446
00:36:31,040 --> 00:36:32,720
About never taking things from the outside.

447
00:36:32,800 --> 00:36:34,240
And there's legal reasons, right?

448
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:36,320
There's risks, so you just identified there.

449
00:36:36,880 --> 00:36:44,480
But like folks always like ignore, they over index on the risk of the startup that goes out of business, which can be mitigated, right, with a couple easy checks.

450
00:36:44,560 --> 00:36:51,200
Like I'll say this for folks evaluating RevenueCat or any tool, if the company is not public, if they're public, you can just look at their financial position, right?

451
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:52,720
You can see how much cash they have.

452
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:54,720
You can kind of understand their solvency.

453
00:36:54,960 --> 00:36:56,960
If they're private, if it's a startup, ask.

454
00:36:57,200 --> 00:36:59,680
And if the salesperson won't tell you, say, like, let me talk to your founder.

455
00:36:59,840 --> 00:37:01,600
I want to know what, like, how much money you guys have.

456
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:10,160
We do fewer of those conversations now, but in the early days, I was like, had to get on a lot of phone calls and be like, listen, I only have seven hundred thousand dollars in the bank, but here's my plan, right?

457
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:12,000
Like we're settled.

458
00:37:12,160 --> 00:37:15,200
Like if I had to, you know, if we needed to lean back, we'd be profitable.

459
00:37:15,360 --> 00:37:19,520
Like the business is, you know, which hasn't always been the case for a lot of dev tools, right?

460
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:24,480
It's really good to hear that you've opened up to that because like, I think a lot of people do themselves a service.

461
00:37:24,800 --> 00:37:33,360
Like, the whole economy since the Middle Ages is based on, not even pre Middle Ages, since basically the Agrarian Revolution has been based on specialization, right?

462
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:38,560
And we've entered this period where with software, it can happen in a different way, right?

463
00:37:38,800 --> 00:37:44,240
Like, specialized crafts people can be trading information in real time and then measuring and exchanging that.

464
00:37:44,480 --> 00:37:50,640
And that's kind of what's caused the explosion in value creation that we've seen over the last two thousand or ten thousand years.

465
00:37:50,800 --> 00:37:55,840
So to deny that and be like, oh, we're going to build everything in house, like denying something that's been working really well.

466
00:37:56,560 --> 00:37:58,400
Again, conditioned on the risks, right?

467
00:37:58,640 --> 00:38:00,160
Conditioned on doing what's right for your business.

468
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:04,720
And then also you learn how to do good vendor integration, right?

469
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:06,160
Where you're defending yourself a little bit.

470
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:07,840
You're being like, yeah, we're going to put this in.

471
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:10,400
We're going to isolate it a little bit so that we have that optionality.

472
00:38:10,880 --> 00:38:16,320
If this vendor doesn't work out for us, we can easily switch over to another or we can bring it in house if we have to and stuff like that.

473
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:21,520
Your RD dollars should be going into IP, which beats the market, right?

474
00:38:21,840 --> 00:38:29,920
Which is creating extra margin and extra value for your customers because just solving the basic needs of the business probably isn't good going to bring a big return for that dollar.

475
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:30,960
Absolutely.

476
00:38:31,120 --> 00:38:34,400
And I have a lot of scars to prove that it took me a long time to learn that.

477
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:58,720
One of the frustrating things about the subscription app industry, specifically, you said you should just go with best in class tools, but so many of the best in class tools that I would generally recommend to folks just starting out, you got to start off with like a $30,000, $50,000 year enterprise plan to even start integrating the tool.

478
00:38:59,280 --> 00:39:03,040
And I wish some of these bigger, kind of best in class companies like Arible.

479
00:39:03,520 --> 00:39:10,000
and Braze on the customer journey and customer data platform side and even some of the bigger like MMPs and other things like that.

480
00:39:10,320 --> 00:39:17,360
I wish there were more companies open to that kind of let us start with you and grow with you instead of like you can't even implement the solution.

481
00:39:17,760 --> 00:39:18,960
I never, I did, but I do not want to do that.

482
00:39:19,280 --> 00:39:21,120
I don't think we lose money on our long tail.

483
00:39:21,280 --> 00:39:22,080
Like don't tell me.

484
00:39:23,840 --> 00:39:26,000
Because I do it out of a moral imperative, right?

485
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:28,560
I have the belief that long term it will net out, right?

486
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:42,880
But many of these companies, they just because typically those 50 K and up, those enterprise contracts, like you can build a go to market motion around that and it's typically pretty efficient and relatively easy to grow compared to, like, a bottom-up motion might take forever and be very costly.

487
00:39:43,040 --> 00:39:43,120
Yeah.

488
00:39:43,680 --> 00:39:53,040
And Twilio is a good example though, like, there are still tools out there that charge on unit economics that can work if you're delivering value, making it, you know, you scale with them.

489
00:39:53,200 --> 00:39:56,080
You don't have to sign a $60,000 plan just to get started.

490
00:39:56,560 --> 00:39:59,600
But yeah, I wish everyone could just start with best in class tools.

491
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:00,640
So it's a trade-off.

492
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:01,520
Mission to win.

493
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:02,320
Really early.

494
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:22,720
You can't always start with best in class tools, but it's cool to hear that you're actually still using Firebase because that's something a lot of folks start with and then eventually move on to a mixed panel, an amplitude, a segment or something like that, but at least there are tools like Firebase where maybe they're not best in class at everything, but they're good enough to take Talking Parents to ten million in ARR.

495
00:40:23,280 --> 00:40:23,680
Absolutely.

496
00:40:23,920 --> 00:40:42,800
I think the key thing there for those that can't necessarily get the best in class in the beginning because you just can't afford to do it is just follow good software engineering techniques in the way that you can actually pull stuff out as you go because the app you are when you're in your first year versus your second or third year, you're going to iterate several times.

497
00:40:43,200 --> 00:40:47,120
If I had a dollar for every time we changed around technology or kind of did something.

498
00:40:47,840 --> 00:40:53,360
And I think that was the benefit of us going back and reengineering it in the first place was it allowed us to do that quicker.

499
00:40:53,680 --> 00:40:55,440
I'm not saying it's not problematic.

500
00:40:55,840 --> 00:40:56,080
It is.

501
00:40:56,320 --> 00:41:07,760
There are certain paradigms that we haven't been able to pull out, but we were able to integrate certain things in and then switch them out because we designed them properly so that if we had to go from one transcription service to another, we could.

502
00:41:08,320 --> 00:41:11,040
If we went from one video provider to another, we could.

503
00:41:11,680 --> 00:41:14,160
And what some founders do is they get in a big hurry.

504
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:18,400
It's hurry up and get it to market as quickly as I can, which I understand.

505
00:41:19,360 --> 00:41:22,080
But at our point, we kind of already knew that we had a market and a fit.

506
00:41:22,640 --> 00:41:29,280
And I think as we were going through, we were making sure that what we could do would scale and that we could change things out as we moved along.

507
00:41:29,920 --> 00:41:36,480
We do need to start wrapping up, but I wanted to do a quick lightning round because I think you have some really insightful points here with Talking Parents.

508
00:41:36,640 --> 00:41:43,360
I know customer service is super important and I don't think enough apps take that super seriously.

509
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:53,040
So give us the 90 seconds, why do you care about support and how does that actually translate into how you run your business and how your customers respond?

510
00:41:53,440 --> 00:42:00,160
Well, I'll say first and foremost that I think that every experience that someone has with your brand sets a mental image of who you are in it.

511
00:42:00,400 --> 00:42:05,280
And so, you know, if I'm going to charge a premium price, then they need to have premium support, right?

512
00:42:05,920 --> 00:42:19,760
One of the things that we've done that I think is absolutely important to us, and we didn't do it in the beginning, and we've only been doing it in the last year and a half, is that on app reviews, we go in and reply to every app review with a human response, right?

513
00:42:20,080 --> 00:42:26,400
Even if someone's just complaining just to complain or they're complaining about their co-parent relationship or something, has nothing to do with the app.

514
00:42:27,040 --> 00:42:33,280
But I want my people, I have my marketing team meets once a week with my customer experience team, and they respond to all of that those.

515
00:42:34,160 --> 00:42:38,080
And obviously, Google gives us less characters than, say, Apple does.

516
00:42:38,960 --> 00:42:40,960
But people then feel like they're heard.

517
00:42:41,200 --> 00:42:43,840
And you're not writing it to answer just that person.

518
00:42:44,720 --> 00:42:47,360
You're writing it for everybody that comes behind it.

519
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:56,720
And if you've got a thoughtful, reasonable answer that empathizes with that particular person, then there's a greater chance that they're going to come back and they're going to think highly of your app.

520
00:42:57,280 --> 00:42:59,360
And I would say we do the same thing with our app.

521
00:42:59,680 --> 00:43:01,440
It's email support only right now.

522
00:43:01,920 --> 00:43:06,640
But one of the things that we're thinking about doing for our higher tiers is giving a phone support.

523
00:43:07,440 --> 00:43:10,880
And if we do that, we'll add that in and treat it with the same care.

524
00:43:11,600 --> 00:43:18,560
So our thing is about radical focus on the customer because again, as I said before, they're the ones that are paying us for the service.

525
00:43:19,280 --> 00:43:26,640
And so if they feel like they're heard and that we're listening to them, they're going to continue to stay on subscription with us and we're going to have great retention rates.

526
00:43:26,960 --> 00:43:27,520
It's amazing.

527
00:43:27,680 --> 00:43:29,600
David, this is the whole point of this whole project.

528
00:43:30,080 --> 00:43:32,560
People making software that helps people and then paying them for it.

529
00:43:32,800 --> 00:43:33,440
It's the dream.

530
00:43:33,680 --> 00:43:34,560
That's all we want, right?

531
00:43:34,880 --> 00:43:38,160
It goes back to the, you know, your customers who pay you.

532
00:43:38,320 --> 00:43:39,040
It's true, right?

533
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:42,720
And reflecting on what you just said, Vince, about our own support, we provide email support.

534
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:43,280
to anyone.

535
00:43:43,600 --> 00:43:44,000
Just sign up.

536
00:43:44,080 --> 00:43:46,480
You'll never make us a dollar all the way up to our biggest customers.

537
00:43:46,640 --> 00:43:55,920
Obviously, there's some gradations there and we kind of like adjust resourcing depending, but we always want to get back to people within a few hours, no matter if they're just lost and trying to figure out how to do it.

538
00:43:56,160 --> 00:43:59,920
And the reason being is because it goes back to what you said, it's it builds that brand, right?

539
00:44:00,160 --> 00:44:16,960
It's like we're establishing a relationship with a community of users and how you react to them and how you're able to treat them, whether it's visible or not or whatever they'll tell their friends really matters and really compounds, especially when you're serving like you guys a somewhat niche community, you have to maintain that.

540
00:44:17,280 --> 00:44:24,800
that like people have to have a high NPS of talking parents if you want your business to take off in a community where people are probably exchanging information a lot.

541
00:44:25,120 --> 00:44:27,520
So that's why I don't look at the unit economics.

542
00:44:27,680 --> 00:44:29,040
I don't want to know, right?

543
00:44:29,360 --> 00:44:35,200
Like, yeah, you were talking earlier, Vence, about making sure on tooling, you know, the unit economics.

544
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:41,520
And again, I think too many apps sleep on how important customer success can be to build a great app business.

545
00:44:41,840 --> 00:44:42,960
And so I think it's really great.

546
00:44:43,200 --> 00:44:45,120
That's a huge focus for talking parents.

547
00:44:45,520 --> 00:44:55,600
And even if it is a quote, unquote, money loser in theer in the long run.

548
00:44:55,680 --> 00:44:57,440
So it's really cool that you're focused on that.

549
00:44:57,920 --> 00:45:00,960
Yeah, I consider it table stakes if you're doing business.

550
00:45:01,200 --> 00:45:02,560
Yeah, it's a cost of doing business.

551
00:45:02,960 --> 00:45:07,440
And when we hire people, we try to look for people that have a lot of empathy, because it's a tough job, right?

552
00:45:07,600 --> 00:45:07,920
To be a business.

553
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:09,520
I can imagine the general public.

554
00:45:10,400 --> 00:45:14,000
We complain about, you know, iOS developers sometimes being cranky and whatnot.

555
00:45:14,240 --> 00:45:20,240
I can't imagine like people already potentially where they're coming from, why they ended up in that app, and then they're having a frustrating moment.

556
00:45:20,800 --> 00:45:25,280
Like, I, I have to imagine your customer experience team has to be a very special kind of person.

557
00:45:25,600 --> 00:45:25,760
Yeah.

558
00:45:25,920 --> 00:45:40,080
And I think there's an altruisticc notion to this.

